The west coast is the sunny and sheltered side of Mauritius and a good base for exploring the whole island. Here is what is useful to know before you go.

## When to come

Mauritius has two main seasons.

- **Summer from November to April** is hot and humid with average day highs around 29 °C in January and February. It is also the rainier season and tropical cyclones can affect the island (mostly between January and March). They are tracked well in advance. If one approaches follow local advice.
- **Winter from June to September** is cooler and drier with nights around 16 °C in July and August. The southeast trade winds are at their strongest and that is what makes Le Morne a kitesurfing destination.

The west coast is drier and sunnier than the east and the central plateau which can be noticeably cooler and wetter. The sea in the lagoons stays between about 22 °C and 27 °C all year.

## Getting there

International flights arrive at Sir Seewoosagur Ramgoolam International Airport (MRU) at Plaine Magnien in the southeast of the island about 48 km from Port Louis. Air Mauritius is based there and several international airlines fly in from Europe and Africa and Asia and the Middle East.

From the airport the west coast is roughly an hour to an hour and a quarter by car depending on where you stay and on traffic.

## Entry requirements

Many visitors (including those from the European Union and the United Kingdom and South Africa) can enter for a holiday without applying for a visa in advance. Rules depend on your nationality and change from time to time. Check with the Mauritius Passport and Immigration Office or your airline before you travel.

## Getting around

- **Driving** is on the left. Renting a car is the easiest way to explore the west and the rest of the island. Roads in the hills (Chamarel and Plaine Champagne) are narrow and winding.
- **Taxis** are easy to find at hotels and in villages; agree the fare before you set off.
- **Buses** connect the coastal villages with the towns of the centre and Port Louis. They are cheap but slower and service is thinner in the evening.

## Money

The currency is the Mauritian rupee (MUR). Cards are widely accepted in hotels and supermarkets and larger restaurants and there are cash machines in the larger villages. Carry some cash for markets and small shops and beach stalls.

## Language

Mauritius has no official language in its constitution. English and French are used in government and schools and business and almost everyone speaks Mauritian Creole at home. Visitors get by easily in English or French.

## Practical details

- **Time zone:** UTC+4 all year (no daylight saving time).
- **Electricity:** 230 V and 50 Hz. Sockets are type G (as in the UK) and type C (the European plug with two round pins).
- **Phone code:** +230.
- **Emergencies:** police 112 or 999. Ambulance (SAMU) 114. Fire 115.

## Respect the island

- Protect the reef: do not stand on coral or take shells from the lagoon and use a reef safe sunscreen.
- Dress modestly away from the beach and especially in temples and churches and mosques.
- Le Morne is a place of remembrance: treat the mountain and the monument with respect.
